gical technique entities—CURETTAGE AND ELECTRODESICCATION and PDEMA/MOHS SURGERY—are explicitly listed as types of surgery for squamous cell skin cancer, indicating technique selection within the surgical modality rather than a surgery-versus-non-surgery decision. [Data: Entities (743, 744); Relationships (1063, 1064)]\\n\\nThe CANCEROUS LESION entity is directly linked to surgery as the target of removal, clarifying the immediate objective of these interventions in regional disease management: reducing tumor burden by physically removing the lesion. [Data: Entities (847); Relationships (1242)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Resectability is a key gatekeeping concept that determines whether surgery can achieve complete tumor removal\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community defines a RESECTABLE TUMOR as one that can be removed completely with surgery and links the term RESECTABLE to the same concept of complete surgical removability. This positions resectability as a categorical decision point that governs feasibility and expected completeness of surgical treatment. [Data: Entities (1650, 2586, 16); Relationships (2733, 4725)]\\n\\nBecause resectability is tied to the tumor’s location/extent, it implicitly determines when surgery is intended to be curative/local control versus when alternative strategies (or combinations) are necessary. The network reinforces this by linking surgery to “removal of cancer from the body” as a stated goal, consistent with complete excision when feasible. [Data: Entities (989); Relationships (1499, 2733)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Surgery is frequently depicted as part of multimodal therapy rather than a stand-alone intervention\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community explicitly represents treatment combination as an operational norm: other treatments may be used with surgery to improve results. This indicates that surgical pathways often integrate adjuvant or neoadjuvant modalities, making coordination across services a core capability rather than an exception. [Data: Entities (990, 16); Relationships (1500)]\\n\\nThis multimodal framing is reinforced by disease-linked relationships where surgery is presented alongside systemic therapy, such as HIGH-GRADE SEROUS CARCINOMA being treated with surgery (and chemotherapy), and non-metastatic colon cancer management being organized around surgery with chemotherapy sometimes given after surgery. [Data: Entities (2172, 4458); Relationships (3720, 9383)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Major organ-removal surgery is represented by bladder removal (partial vs radical cystectomy) for advanced disease\",\n      \"explanation\": \"BLADDER REMOVAL is defined as a surgical approach for bladder cancer performed via cystectomy, including partial (removing part of the bladder) and radical (removing the entire bladder) procedures, with radical cystectomy emphasized for more advanced cases. This highlights a high-acuity surgical category with significant implications for care planning and resource needs. [Data: Entities (5759); Relationships (13002)]\\n\\nThe explicit linkage between surgery and bladder removal situates cystectomy within the broader surgical decision network, indicating that “surgery” in this community includes not only local lesion excision but also extensive organ-removal operations when indicated. [Data: Entities (16, 5759); Relationships (13002)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical risk and operational constraints are emphasized: comorbidities, severe complications, and difficult removal scenarios\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community flags that OTHER HEALTH CONDITIONS can complicate head and neck cancer surgery and that preparation for what to expect after head and neck surgery helps prevent SEVERE COMPLICATIONS. Together, these relationships stress that surgical impact is not limited to technical execution—patient selection, perioperative optimization, and expectation-setting are treated as risk